WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:22.335
One fall afternoon Mr. Harrison and Anne were having afternoon tea when Mr. Harrison inquired, "Been writing any more stories lately, Anne?"

2
00:00:22.336 --> 00:00:25.764
"No," answered Anne rather curtly.

3
00:00:25.765 --> 00:00:34.244
"I heard that someone recently sent a big envelope to the Rollings Reliable Baking Company in Montreal," remarked Mr. Harrison.

4
00:00:34.245 --> 00:00:39.829
"I thought maybe it was you, sending in your story to the Rollings Reliable Story Competition."

5
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:44.074
"I would never do that!" replied Anne indignantly.

6
00:00:44.075 --> 00:00:51.327
"I read about the competition, but I think it's disgraceful to write a story to advertise a baking powder.

7
00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:57.277
It's almost as bad as Mr. Parker's fence with advertising all over it!"

8
00:00:57.278 --> 00:01:01.739
When Anne said that, she had no idea of how the day would unfold.

9
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.870
That very evening, Diana popped into Green Gables. She was carrying a letter.

10
00:01:07.871 --> 00:01:11.947
"Anne, open this letter right away!" she said excitedly.

11
00:01:11.948 --> 00:01:14.985
"I was at the post office, so I picked it up for you.

12
00:01:14.986 --> 00:01:18.243
I have a feeling it's good news."

13
00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:23.210
Puzzled, Anne opened the letter. "Dear Madam," she read.

14
00:01:23.211 --> 00:01:34.381
"We take much pleasure in informing you that your charming story 'Averil's Atonement' has won the first prize of $25 in our recent story competition.

15
00:01:34.382 --> 00:01:38.778
Regards, Rollings Reliable Baking Company.

16
00:01:38.779 --> 00:01:42.379
I don't understand," said Anne blankly.

17
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.954
Diana clapped her hands. "Oh, I knew it would win the prize! I was sure of it.

18
00:01:47.955 --> 00:01:51.232
I sent your story into the competition, Anne."

19
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:54.707
"Diana Barry!" Anne exclaimed.

20
00:01:54.708 --> 00:02:00.834
Diana was not always the most observant person, but she realized that Anne was not overjoyed.

21
00:02:00.835 --> 00:02:04.313
"Why, Anne, you don't seem a bit pleased."

22
00:02:04.314 --> 00:02:13.347
Anne instantly made herself smile. "Diana," Anne began, "of course I'm touched that you helped me get my story published.

23
00:02:13.348 --> 00:02:24.833
That was very unselfish of you, but . . . there wasn't a word in my story about . . . about . . ." Anne choked a little over the word, "baking powder!"

24
00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:33.045
"Oh, I put that in," said Diana. "It was easy! You know the scene where Averil makes the cake?

25
00:02:33.046 --> 00:02:39.234
Well, I just added in that she used Rollings Reliable Baking Powder, and that's why the cake was so delicious.

26
00:02:39.235 --> 00:02:42.901
And in the final scene, after Averil and Harold kiss . . ."

27
00:02:42.902 --> 00:02:46.642
"Yes?" said Anne quietly.

28
00:02:46.643 --> 00:02:55.231
"I wrote, 'and they always used Rollings Reliable Baking Powder in every cake, and lived happily ever after,'" Diana quoted proudly.

29
00:02:55.232 --> 00:03:00.219
"Oh," gasped Anne, as if someone had thrown cold water on her.

30
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.366
"And you've won twenty-five dollars," continued Diana jubilantly.

31
00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:11.972
"Why, I heard that magazines only pay five dollars a story! I'm so happy for you, Anne."

32
00:03:11.973 --> 00:03:15.878
Anne held the hateful check that had been attached to the letter.

33
00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:26.530
"I can't take it, Diana. You deserve it, since you sent the story in and made the changes. I . . . I wouldn't have sent it."

34
00:03:26.531 --> 00:03:31.530
"Absolutely not! I would never take that money; it's yours, Anne!

35
00:03:31.531 --> 00:03:36.864
The honor of being a friend of the prizewinner is enough for me," Diana said.

36
00:03:36.865 --> 00:03:42.259
Anne suddenly bent forward, put her arms around Diana, and kissed her cheek.

37
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:49.131
"I think you are the sweetest and truest friend in the world, Diana. Thank you."

38
00:03:49.132 --> 00:03:51.895
Anne left with tears in her eyes.

39
00:03:51.896 --> 00:03:56.663
She felt deeply embarrassed over what had happened to her literary endeavor.

40
00:03:56.664 --> 00:04:03.051
"I will never live this down," she thought. "Never!"

41
00:04:03.052 --> 00:04:06.371
A week later Anne left for Halifax.

42
00:04:06.372 --> 00:04:12.747
For the first time, she was not sorry to leave Avonlea because her last week there had not been pleasant.

43
00:04:12.748 --> 00:04:18.980
Her story was published in all the newspapers on Prince Edward Island, and everyone had an opinion.

44
00:04:18.981 --> 00:04:30.239
Josie Pye was sure that Anne had copied the story while Mrs. Lynde said, unimpressed, "It's perfectly amazing, the price they pay for such lies!"

45
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:37.224
Back in Halifax Anne, Priscilla, Stella, and Phil settled quickly into their new home at Patty's Place.

46
00:04:37.225 --> 00:04:47.143
Mrs. Lynde and Marilla had given Anne jams and cakes and other foods, so the girls had a full pantry. And soon they were welcoming visitors.

47
00:04:47.144 --> 00:04:53.985
They passed many enjoyable Friday evenings entertaining guests around the fireplace in their cozy living room.

48
00:04:53.986 --> 00:04:59.552
Gilbert was a frequent visitor, but Anne still felt uncomfortable around him at times.

49
00:04:59.553 --> 00:05:03.554
It seemed as if their relationship had gotten very complicated.

50
00:05:03.555 --> 00:05:12.464
One afternoon Anne was sitting under the apple tree behind Patty's Place when she saw Gilbert coming toward her with a bunch of wildflowers.

51
00:05:12.465 --> 00:05:17.616
"Don't these remind you of home and our old school picnics, Anne?" he asked.

52
00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:21.025
Anne smiled and buried her face in the flowers.

53
00:05:21.026 --> 00:05:25.001
"I feel like I'm back in Avonlea right now!"

54
00:05:25.002 --> 00:05:28.864
"Will you go back to Avonlea for winter vacation?" Gilbert asked.

55
00:05:28.865 --> 00:05:33.779
"Yes, I will," Anne answered. "And I guess you will too?"

56
00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.747
"No, I'll be working in Halifax," said Gilbert.

57
00:05:37.748 --> 00:05:46.009
"Oh," said Anne, surprised. She couldn't picture what a whole Avonlea winter would be like without Gilbert.

58
00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:53.511
"Well, a new job . . . that's exciting," said Anne, without any very clear idea of what she was saying.

59
00:05:53.512 --> 00:05:57.165
Gilbert was now staring at Anne with a strange expression.

60
00:05:57.166 --> 00:06:01.174
Anne wished desperately that Phil would come out and interrupt them.

61
00:06:01.175 --> 00:06:08.334
"Well . . . uh . . . I found a cluster of white violets under that old twisted tree today," Anne mumbled.

62
00:06:08.335 --> 00:06:13.258
"They were so pretty, I felt as if I'd discovered a gold mine!"

63
00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:17.476
"You are always discovering gold mines, Anne," said Gilbert.

64
00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:26.017
"Let's find some more violets. I think I saw some more over there," suggested Anne quickly. "I'll call Phil and—"

65
00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:31.633
"Never mind Phil and the violets just now, Anne," said Gilbert, quietly taking her hand.

66
00:06:31.634 --> 00:06:37.059
He looked deep into Anne's eyes. "There is something I want to say to you."

67
00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:43.496
"Oh, don't say it," cried Anne. "Don't . . . please, Gilbert," she begged him.

68
00:06:43.497 --> 00:06:49.521
"I must," Gilbert said firmly. "Things can't go on like this any longer.

69
00:06:49.522 --> 00:06:55.386
Anne, I love you. You know I do. I can't tell you how much.

70
00:06:55.387 --> 00:07:05.454
Will you promise me that one day you'll be my wife?"
